//
// P245 regression guard — `parallel { }` arms must observe outer-scope
// variables at their declared values.  Pre-fix the worker's stack was
// empty when the arm body ran, so reads at the parent's frame offsets
// returned 0 / garbage; arm bodies that called fns with outer-scope
// args (`server_arm(port)`) saw the args as 0.

// Forwards `n` so the parallel arm has work to do; the arm-body
// codegen reads `n` (the call argument) from the parent's frame
// position — this is the read that returned 0 pre-P245-fix.
//
// ⚠ The assertion below does NOT fail the run.  A runtime fault raised inside a
// `parallel` arm — assert or panic — is silently discarded on both backends
// (loft#1053): falsifying this line still exits 0.  It is kept because it states the
// arm's contract at the place the contract applies, but it is NOT the guard.  The
// enforceable half is `main`'s queue-route cell below, which routes the SAME read
// through a body that names the result, so the parent can assert on what the arm saw.
fn forward(n: integer) -> integer {
  assert(n == 42, "arm received outer var unchanged: got {n}");
  n
}

fn main() {
  outer = 42;
  parallel {
    forward(outer);
    forward(outer);
  }
  // After the parallel block completes, the parent's outer is
  // unchanged (each arm's writes go to its own worker stack).
  assert(outer == 42, "outer var preserved across parallel block");

  // The ENFORCEABLE half of the P245 guarantee.  `parallel { … }` discards its arms'
  // results, so nothing the arm observed can reach the parent and an in-arm assertion
  // is currently the only place to look — which loft#1053 makes silent.  The queue form
  // names the result, so the value the worker SAW comes back and is checked here, where
  // an assertion does fail the run.  Verified to bite: changing `== 42` fails the file.
  rows: vector<integer> = [];
  for i in 0..2 { rows += [i]; }
  seen = 0;
  total = 0;
  for r in rows par(saw = forward_outer(r, outer), 2) {
    total += saw;
    seen += 1;
  }
  assert(seen == 2, "queue route expected 2 rows, got {seen}");
  assert(total == 84, "each arm must observe outer==42, so 42+42; got {total}");
}

// The same read as `forward`, with the outer var passed as an argument the worker
// returns — so the parent can see what the arm saw.
fn forward_outer(row: integer, n: integer) -> integer {
  n + row * 0
}
